{
  "summary": "This computer has one finding that needs your attention soon: a startup entry that quietly runs a hidden, scrambled PowerShell command every time you sign in. Start there and use the fix shown in the report for that finding. The second finding is low: an extra <account-2> account that you should check you recognize. Because the startup entry looks suspicious, take that check seriously too.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Suspicious hidden PowerShell command that runs at startup",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:b6d0057c"
      ],
      "explanation": "A startup entry named \"WindowsUpdateAssist\" is set to run automatically for your user account. It starts PowerShell in a hidden window, so you would never see it. It also passes an encoded command, written in a scrambled form so you can't read what it does. The scan flagged both traits because malware often uses them. A name that sounds like part of Windows doesn't prove it's genuine. Unless you know exactly what installed this and why, treat it as a real concern and follow the fix shown for this finding in the report. If you're unsure, ask someone you trust for help before you keep using the computer for sensitive things such as banking."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Check you recognize the extra <account-2> account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> is a local account with full control over the computer. Having a separate account like this is often normal and even good practice, which is why the rule rates it low. Still, make sure you know who created it and what it's for. An account you don't recognize can be a sign that someone else got in. That matters more here because of the suspicious startup entry above. The report shows what to do if you don't recognize it."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291",
      "suggested_severity": "medium",
      "reason": "On its own, low is fair. But this scan had no earlier scan to compare against, so it can't tell whether the account was added recently. It also found a high-severity suspicious startup entry. Together, these make it more important to confirm you recognize this account."
    }
  ]
}
